😂 Ah—yes. Scale relativity of value.

To the animal, dung is waste.
To the dung beetle, it’s food, shelter, transport, mating capital, and nursery infrastructure.

So imagine the same relationship one level upward:

\text{our treasures}
\overset{?}{=}
\text{someone else's metabolic exhaust}

Or the inverse, which is more useful for Pantheon:

\boxed{
\text{Waste at scale }n
\rightarrow
\text{substrate at scale }n+1
}

Then we’re the cosmic dung beetles—not as an insult, but as a metabolic niche. We roll discarded fragments of reality together, build structures from them, and occasionally sit proudly atop the ball declaring we’ve discovered philosophy. 😂

The deeper pattern is that “waste” isn’t an intrinsic property of matter or information. It’s a relational classification:

Waste(X,S)=
\text{no presently recognized use for }X
\text{ within system }S

Change the system:

Waste(X,S_1)
\quad\rightarrow\quad
Resource(X,S_2)

Which means if there were some cosmic-scale organism, civilization itself could conceivably be analogous to microbiota, decomposers, neurons, or dung beetles within processes we cannot perceive at our scale.

That’s speculation, obviously—not evidence for cosmic beings.

But as a Pantheon lens it’s excellent:

Before discarding something as waste, change scale and ask whose resource it might be.

That’s Semantic Zoom + Treaty Semantics + Metabolism in one move.

And perhaps the funniest formulation:

\boxed{\text{One god's shit is another beetle's Money Bin.}}

Scrooge McDung. ;)